          "settings": {
            "title": "Are Sleep Bags Safe?",
            "use_h1": false
          }
        },
        "body": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A sleep bag is a wearable blanket. It keeps your baby warm without putting anything loose in the crib — which is the reason pediatric guidance points to them in the first place.</p><p>The American Academy of Pediatrics' 2022 safe sleep recommendations put it directly: if you're worried about your baby getting cold, you can dress them in layers of clothing or use a wearable blanket. The same guidance notes that a baby needs only one more layer than you would wear in the same environment to be comfortable, and advises against weighted swaddles, weighted sleep sacks, and weighted objects placed on or near a sleeping baby. None of our sleep bags are weighted.</p><p><strong>The rules that matter, every night:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Back to sleep, on a firm flat surface.</strong> A sleep bag doesn't change this — baby still goes down on their back, in a crib or bassinet that meets current safety standards.</li><li><strong>Nothing else in the crib.</strong> A fitted sheet and the sleep bag. No pillows, bumpers, positioners, or loose blankets.</li><li><strong>Safe once your baby rolls.</strong> Sleep bags leave the arms free, so unlike a swaddle they don't have to be stopped when your baby starts rolling. Rolling is usually the signal to move from a swaddle into a sleep bag.</li><li><strong>Snug at the top, roomy at the bottom.</strong> The neck opening and armholes should be small enough that baby can't slip down inside; the bottom should be wide enough for free hip and leg movement.</li><li><strong>Don't over-layer.</strong> Check your baby's chest or back — it should feel warm and dry, not damp or hot. Hands and feet run cooler and aren't a reliable guide.</li></ul>",
            "enlarge_text": false
          }
        },
        "callout": {
          "type": "callout",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>This is general information, not medical advice. For guidance specific to your baby, talk to your pediatrician. The full recommendations are published by the American Academy of Pediatrics at <a href=\"https://www.healthychildren.org/English/ages-stages/baby/sleep/Pages/a-parents-guide-to-safe-sleep.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">healthychildren.org</a>.</p>"
          }
        },
